 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Replacement Policy (2Q)
+
+The cache keeps two queues over its entries, both private to the worker:
+
+- **First-use queue**: FIFO. A sheet enters here when its render completes and is admitted.
+- **Reuse queue**: LRU. A sheet moves here from the first-use queue when a request hits it again, and moves to the most-recent end on each later hit.
+
+When admission needs room, eviction takes from the oldest end of the first-use queue while that queue holds more than its configured share of the 12 GiB budget; otherwise it takes from the least-recently-used end of the reuse queue. A long sequential screening therefore churns through the first-use queue and does not displace sheets that editors have revisited.
+
+The first-use share is a configuration value local to the worker. The queues and share are not visible through the preview interface, the render queue, or any other node.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Replacement policy | 2Q: first-use FIFO queue plus reuse LRU queue | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Editors alternate long sequential screenings with repeated comparison of a small set. LRU lets a screening evict comparison sheets. Aged frequency counts keep stale favorites and add counter maintenance. 2Q protects reused sheets from one-off scans for the cost of two queues and one share parameter. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. Initial value of the first-use share, and whether it is tuned from observed pilot traffic.
+2. Whether recently evicted first-use keys are remembered so a quick second request is still recognized as reuse.
+3. Whether queue membership survives a worker restart or the cache starts cold.
